So when TTY width is 40, the format is the "same line" style (wrapped), and ARGS also uses the same-line style with indent 10. So the "next_line_help" mode is only used when there's enough width? Hmm, no — clap uses next_line_help when the help text doesn't fit... Actually it's the opposite: clap uses next-line-help when the arg name is long (so the help would start too far right). clap's logic in `write_arg`:

```rust
fn should_show_arg(use_long, arg) -> bool ...
fn write_args(&mut self, args: &[&Arg<'help>], _category: &str, sort_key: fn(&Arg) -> (usize, String)) {
    ...
    let next_line_help = self.map_long_help(args); // ?
}
```

Hmm, let me look at clap_builder 4.6.0's help.rs to understand. Actually, in clap 4 the logic is:

```rust
    /// Will use next line help on writing args.
    fn use_long_help(&self) -> bool {
        debug!("HelpTemplate::use_long_help");
        // In long help mode, if any argument has a long help we should use that
        let mut should_long = true;
        ...
    }
```

Hmm, I recall:
```rust
fn write_args(&mut self, args, _category, sort_key) {
    ...
    let next_line_help = self.map_long_help();
    ...
}
fn map_long_help(&mut self) -> bool {
   ...
}
```

Actually in clap 4.x there's:
```rust
    fn write_args(&mut self, args: &[&Arg], _category: &str, sort_key: ...) {
        let mut longest = 2;
        let mut ord: Vec<&Arg> = ...
        let next_line_help = self.will_args_wrap(args, longest);
        ...
    }
```
where `will_args_wrap` returns true if any arg's help would be wrapped given the available width. Specifically:

```rust
    fn will_args_wrap(&self, args: &[&Arg], longest: usize) -> bool {
        args.iter()
            .filter(|arg| should_show_arg(self.use_long, arg))
            .any(|arg| {
                let spec_vals = &self.spec_vals(arg);
                self.arg_next_line_help(arg, spec_vals, longest)
            })
    }

    fn arg_next_line_help(&self, arg: &Arg, spec_vals: &str, longest: usize) -> bool {
        if self.next_line_help | self.use_long || arg.is_next_line_help_set() {
            true
        } else {
            let h = arg.get_help().unwrap_or_default();
            let h_w = h.display_width() + display_width(spec_vals);
            let taken = longest + 12;
            self.term_w >= taken
                && (taken as f32 / self.term_w as f32) > 0.40
                && h_w > (self.term_w - taken)
        }
    }
```

Something like that. In the 40-col case, term_w=40, taken = longest+12; longest = 33 ("--print-template <print-template>"), taken=45 > 40, so `self.term_w >= taken` fails → false → not next line help. In the non-TTY case, term_w = 100 (default), taken = 45, 45/100 = 0.45 > 0.40 ✓, and h_w (e.g., "Force emoji usage even if it might not work on your system" = 57) > 100-45 = 55 ✓ → true → next line help. 

So with the default width 100, we get next-line help. This is exactly clap's built-in behavior, so if I declare the same args, clap will produce the same output. 

So using clap is the way to go — it'll automatically match. I need to determine the exact clap version (4.6.1 is in the registry; clap_builder 4.6.0). Let me just use clap 4.6.1.

Now: the "OPTIONS:" section order must match declaration order (clap preserves declaration order by default). And -h/--help and -V/--version are declared last (they appear last). If I don't declare them explicitly, clap adds them... In clap 4, `--help` and `--version` are auto-added and appear... hmm, clap adds them via `_build` at the end? Let me test empirically: write a small test program with the same args and see.

