serde_yaml's Number stores the parsed value, so `Real("1.5")` with the original string can't come from serde_yaml.

So crowbook's BookOption is likely parsed from YAML by crowbook's own code using serde_yaml... hmm.

Hmm, hold on. Let me reconsider: maybe crowbook uses the `toml` crate? No, toml's Float Debug is `Float(1.5)`.

Hmm. What about `serde_yaml` 0.9 with `Value::Number`... no.

What if crowbook uses **`yaml` (yaml-rust)**? Its `Yaml` enum:
```rust
pub enum Yaml {
    Real(String),
    String(String),
    Array(Vec<Yaml>),
    Hash(Hash),
    Boolean(bool),
    Null,
    BadValue,
    Integer(i64),
}
```
and Debug is derived → `Real("1.5")`, `Integer(1)`, `Boolean(true)`, `Array([Integer(1), Integer(2)])`, `Null`, `String("foo")`.

That matches EXACTLY! And yaml-rust parses `0x10` as 16? Let me check: yaml-rust's scanner/parser converts plain scalars: it tries integer parsing including hex/octal. In yaml-rust's `parser.rs`, `::insert_scalar` handles: if the string matches an integer pattern → Integer, else if it looks like a float → Real, else String. yaml-rust's `Yaml::from_str`:
```rust
pub fn from_str(v: &str) -> Yaml {
    if v.starts_with("0x") { ... }
}
```
Actually yaml-rust has:
```rust
impl Yaml {
    pub fn from_str(v: &str) -> Yaml {
        if v.starts_with("0x") {
            if let Ok(i) = i64::from_str_radix(&v[2..], 16) { return Yaml::Integer(i); }
        }
        ...
    }
}
